## Why This Is Asked
Tests the ability to combine hash tables with heaps for real-time top-K queries under sliding-window expirations. It evaluates understanding of lazy cleanup techniques, time-based eviction strategies, and maintaining data consistency under concurrent updates.

## Key Concepts
- Hash map for O(1) task lookups and metadata storage
- Max-heap for efficient top-K ordering
- Lazy deletion to avoid expensive per-update heap removals
- Time-based expiration using timestamp validation

## Code Example
```javascript
class Leaderboard {
  constructor(W) {
    this.W = W;
    this.map = new Map(); // task_id -> {score, timestamp}
    this.heap = new MaxHeap(); // (score, task_id)
  }

  update(task_id, delta, ts) {
    const current = this.map.get(task_id) || { score: 0, timestamp: 0 };
    const updated = { 
      score: current.score + delta, 
      timestamp: ts 
    };
    this.map.set(task_id, updated);
    this.heap.push([updated.score, task_id]);
  }

  getTopK(k, current_ts) {
    const result = [];
    const temp = [];

    while (result.length < k && !this.heap.isEmpty()) {
      const [score, task_id] = this.heap.pop();
      const task = this.map.get(task_id);

      if (task && task.score === score && current_ts - task.timestamp <= this.W) {
        result.push({ task_id, score });
      } else {
        temp.push([score, task_id]);
      }
    }

    // Restore invalid entries
    temp.forEach(entry => this.heap.push(entry));

    return result;
  }
}
```

## Complexity Analysis
- **Update**: O(1) hash map + O(log n) heap push = O(log n)
- **Top-K query**: O(k log n) worst case due to lazy deletion
- **Space**: O(n) for hash map + heap (potentially O(n + m) where m are stale entries)
- **Expiration**: Handled during queries via timestamp validation

## Why This Is Asked

This question probes understanding of introsort-style hybrids, balancing average performance with worst-case guarantees in production environments. It tests pivot strategies, partitioning, depth tracking, and in-place constraints.

## Key Concepts

- Introsort: hybrid of quicksort and heapsort with depth-based switch
- Median-of-three pivot: reduces chance of worst-case partitions
- 3-way partitioning: handles duplicates efficiently
- Insertion sort for tiny subarrays: amortized gains

## Code Example

```javascript
function introsort(arr) {
  const maxDepth = Math.floor(Math.log2(arr.length)) * 2;
  function insertionSort(a, lo, hi) {
    for (let i = lo + 1; i < hi; i++) {
      let key = a[i], j = i - 1;
      while (j >= lo && a[j] > key) { a[j + 1] = a[j]; j--; }
      a[j + 1] = key;
    }
  }
  function heapify(a, count, i, offset) {
    // placeholder for in-place heapify within [offset, offset+count)
  }
  function partition(a, lo, hi) {
    // median-of-three and 3-way partitioning placeholder
    return Math.floor((lo + hi) / 2);
  }
  function sort(a, lo, hi, depth) {
    if (hi - lo <= 16) { insertionSort(a, lo, hi); return; }
    if (depth > maxDepth) { /* heapsort(a, lo, hi); */ return; }
    const pivot = partition(a, lo, hi);
    // recursively sort partitions
    sort(a, lo, pivot, depth + 1);
    sort(a, pivot, hi, depth + 1);
  }
  sort(arr, 0, arr.length, 0);
}
```

